<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <ScenTitle>Operation Desert Falcon</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY&gt;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0in; LINE-HEIGHT: normal"&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;Operation Desert Falcon&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = "o" ns = "urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0in; LINE-HEIGHT: normal"&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0in; LINE-HEIGHT: normal"&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;Date/Time: &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;7th June, 2027/ 20:00:00 Zulu&lt;B&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0in; LINE-HEIGHT: normal"&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;Location: &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;Chad&lt;B&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0in; LINE-HEIGHT: normal"&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;Duration: &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;22 Hours&lt;B&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0in; LINE-HEIGHT: normal"&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;Playable Sides: &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;SPAN style='FONT-FAMILY: "Times New Roman",serif'&gt;France&lt;B&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt" align=center&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;It is 2027. The French empire crumbles.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The collapse has not come by surprise. Relations between France and her former West African holdings had been rapidly declining since the early 2020s. Neither continued French investment, nor cultural and political engagement, nor extensive military support (especially against insurgent groups in the Sahel) proved enough to mitigate the former colonial power’s rising unpopularity in the region. Local leaders, most notably in Mali and in Niger, had begun to rail openly against perceived “neo-colonial arrogance”; young people increasingly saw France not as a force for stability and democracy, but as a “prop for the old guard establishment”. Facing such a backdrop of malcontent, France had already begun a measured drawdown of its regional entanglements, promising a complete pullout in the indeterminate future.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;What caught the French off guard was thus not the decline of its imperial legacy but rather its harbinger: the United Sahel Liberation Front, or USLF. Founded in an unexpected merger between several regional terror groups, the group gained widespread appeal among West African governments and civilians alike by offering “Pan-Africanism with Islamist characteristics”. This promise proved especially intoxicating to young people across the region who saw USLF as an opportunity to throw off the yoke of their sluggish, Western-backed governments. &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;Nowhere was this more pronounced than in Chad, where the extensive French military presence proved a flashpoint for anti-Western sentiment. Chad’s military leadership saw little reason to stay friendly with a French force in retreat; seeing the writing on the wall, they began to cultivate relations with the USLF. In under a year, the Chadian government had all but become a branch of the group, and the home base for its dream of a united Sahel. Many military units defected outright, reportedly with blessings from the Chadian junta; those few troops and civilians who did not support the group had few options but to flee, many into the arms of the departing French vanguard at N'Djamena International Airport.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The small French contingent left behind was not prepared for the rapid collapse of the host government, nor for the extensive humanitarian crisis that arrived literally at their gates. Nor was it prepared to repel the large attack that followed. The night began with several suicide bombings and sporadic mortar strikes but quickly escalated into a major assault against the outgunned French force and the civilians it sheltered. When Chadian forces co-located at the base ran away (or themselves took up arms against their allies) the French contingent had no choice but to abandon the airfield and retreat with some 500 civilians into the nearby city, where they set up a hasty defensive perimeter and prepared for the next wave of attacks.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;At once humiliated and incensed, the French president immediately ordered a major rescue operation and airstrikes against USLF targets across Chad. However, with most of the French military long withdrawn and no time to position forces more advantageously, the burden of carrying Marianne’s torch of liberty fell to the French Air Force in Southern France and a hodgepodge of naval units that happened to be near. French procurement and doctrine long emphasized deep strike capability and African power projection; Operation Desert Falcon, as the daring rescue was christened, would be a vindication of such preparations.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;Scenario Author&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;Jake “Kommissar” Keyes&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: justify; TEXT-JUSTIFY: inter-ideograph; MARGIN: 0in 0in 8pt"&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;Description by Ethan Hermanson&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;</ScenDescription>
